---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/article.css';

const TITLE = 'Privacy Policy | ReflexLab';
const DESCRIPTION =
  'Privacy policy for ReflexLab. Your test results stay in your browser. What gets stored, how analytics and advertising use cookies, and how to opt out.';

// Bump this by hand whenever the policy text changes, and only then. Deriving
// it from the build date would stamp a fresh revision date on every deploy,
// which is exactly the claim a "last updated" line must not make falsely.
const LAST_UPDATED = '19 November 2026';

const EMAIL = 'hello@reflexlab.co';
---

<BaseLayout title={TITLE} description={DESCRIPTION} path="/privacy" topbarLabel="Privacy">
  <article class="article-container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span class="breadcrumbs-separator">/</span>
        <span class="breadcrumbs-current">Privacy Policy</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="article-title">Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="article-subtitle">How ReflexLab handles your data.</p>
      <p class="article-updated">Last updated {LAST_UPDATED}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="article-content">
      <p class="article-intro">
        Your scores stay on your device. ReflexLab has no accounts, no sign-ups, and no server that
        receives your results. Three outside services do handle data when you visit: Google
        Analytics measures traffic, Google AdSense serves ads, and our host logs the requests it
        serves. Each one is described below.
      </p>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>What ReflexLab Stores</h2>
        <p>
          Every test writes to your browser's localStorage under keys beginning with{' '}
          <code>reflexlab:</code>. For each test on the site, that covers:
        </p>
        <ul class="article-list">
          <li>Your personal best, and the settings you set it under</li>
          <li>Your recent run history, with a timestamp on each run</li>
          <li>Which rank tiers or milestones you have reached</li>
          <li>Your settings for that test, such as duration, delay, input method, and sound</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          None of it is transmitted. It stays in the browser profile that wrote it, which also means
          your scores will not follow you to another browser or another device. If you used
          ReflexLab before its rebuild, you may still hold keys beginning{' '}
          <code>reactionlab_</code> or <code>cpslab_</code> from the earlier version of the site. Those
          were imported once so your old personal bests survived, and the Reset button erases them along
          with everything else.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>No Accounts or Sign-Ups</h2>
        <p>
          ReflexLab never asks you to create an account, give an email address, or sign in. Open a
          test and take it. We hold no personal information about you unless you email us.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Deleting Your Data</h2>
        <p>
          Each test has its own Reset button in the toolbar above the test area. It clears that
          test's personal best, run history, trend graph, and achieved ranks, and it leaves your
          settings in place so you do not have to rebuild them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Reset acts on the test you are looking at. Clearing your CPS data leaves your reaction
          time scores untouched. To erase every test at once, clear this site's data through your
          browser settings.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Cookies</h2>
        <p>
          ReflexLab's own code sets no cookies. Your scores and settings live in localStorage, which
          is separate from cookies and never travels with a network request. Google Analytics and
          Google AdSense do set cookies. The next two sections cover what those cookies do and how
          to turn them off.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Analytics</h2>
        <p>
          ReflexLab uses Google Analytics to count page views and see which tests people use. It
          collects the standard set: the pages you visit, your approximate location down to country
          or region, your device type, and your browser. It never receives your scores, which stay
          in localStorage and are never read by the analytics script.
        </p>
        <p>
          The script loads from <code>googletagmanager.com</code> and sets cookies that hold a random
          identifier, so Analytics can tell a repeat visit from a new one. The cookies do not hold your
          name, your email address, or your scores. To opt out, install{' '}
          <a
            href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            Google's opt-out add-on</a
          >, block the domain with an extension, or turn off cookies for this site.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Advertising</h2>
        <p>
          ReflexLab uses Google AdSense for advertising. Third-party vendors, including Google, use
          cookies to serve ads based on your earlier visits to this site and to other websites.
          Google's advertising cookies let Google and its partners serve ads to you based on those
          visits.
        </p>
        <p>
          To serve and measure ads, these vendors collect your IP address, a cookie identifier, your
          device and browser details, and the pages where you saw an ad. ReflexLab sends none of
          your scores or settings to them.
        </p>
        <p>You can turn personalised advertising off:</p>
        <ul class="article-list">
          <li>
            For Google, use{' '}
            <a href="https://www.google.com/settings/ads" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
              Ads Settings</a
            >.
          </li>
          <li>
            For other vendors, use{' '}
            <a href="https://www.aboutads.info/choices/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
              aboutads.info/choices</a
            >.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Opting out stops the personalisation. You will still see ads, chosen from the page you are
          on instead of your browsing history.
        </p>
        <p>
          Google works with other ad technology providers, which may serve or measure ads on this
          site. Google publishes{' '}
          <a
            href="https://support.google.com/adsense/answer/9012903"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">the list of those providers</a
          >, with a link to each provider's privacy policy and the opt-out it offers. For Google's
          own handling of this data, read{' '}
          <a
            href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >How Google uses information from sites or apps that use our services</a
          >{' '}and{' '}
          <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Google's Privacy Policy</a
          >.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Hosting</h2>
        <p>
          Vercel hosts ReflexLab. Like any web host, its servers log the requests they serve, and
          those logs include your IP address, the page you asked for, and your user agent. We do not
          use them to build a profile of you or link them to anything else.
        </p>
        <p>
          See{' '}
          <a
            href="https://vercel.com/legal/privacy-policy"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Vercel's Privacy Policy</a
          > for how they handle that data.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>What We Do Not Do</h2>
        <p>
          We do not ask for your name, run accounts, or keep a profile of you. Nothing you do inside
          a test reaches us. The only personal information ReflexLab itself receives is what you put
          in an email to us.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Children</h2>
        <p>
          ReflexLab is a general-audience site and is not directed at children under 13. We do not
          knowingly collect personal information from children. If you believe a child has provided
          us with personal information, write to <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a> and we will delete
          it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Your Rights</h2>
        <p>
          Depending on where you live, privacy law may give you the right to see, correct, or delete
          personal information held about you. ReflexLab holds none beyond the emails you have sent
          us, and we delete those on request. Google holds the analytics and advertising data, and
          the opt-out links above control it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          ReflexLab runs reflexlab.co and answers for this policy. Send privacy questions and
          requests to <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a>. When you email us, we receive your
          address and your message, and we use them only to reply.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Changes to This Policy</h2>
        <p>
          When what the site does changes, this page changes with it, and the date at the top of the
          page moves to match. Anything material gets written here rather than announced elsewhere.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="disclaimer-section">
    <p class="disclaimer">
      <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> ReflexLab's tests are built for entertainment and personal tracking.
      Your device, browser, input hardware, and display refresh rate all shape the numbers you see. For
      clinical or research measurement, use calibrated laboratory equipment.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article-updated {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-sm);
    font-size: var(--text-label-size);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .disclaimer-section {
    padding: var(--space-xl) 0 0;
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--line-subtle);
  }

  .disclaimer {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    font-size: var(--text-label-size);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .article-content code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    color: var(--ink-primary);
    background: var(--panel-02);
    border: 1px solid var(--line-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
  }
</style>
